    "section": "Ahnika 1",
    "devanagari": "यत्तु ज्ञेयसतत्त्वस्य ज्ञानं सर्वात्मनोज्झितम् ।\nअवच्छेदैर्न तत्कुत्राप्यज्ञानं सत्यमुक्तिदम् ॥ ३५ ॥",
    "iast": "yat tu jñeyasatattvasya jñānaṃ sarvātmanojjhitam |\navacchedair na tat kutrāpy ajñānaṃ satyamuktidam || 35 ||",
    "translation": "But knowledge of the object of knowledge, together with its constituent principles, that is entirely free of limitations in every respect — such that no ignorance remains anywhere in it — this alone bestows true liberation.",
    "commentary": "**Traditional Context**\nThis verse closes the sub-section by stating the positive standard implied by verse 34: true liberation requires knowledge so complete that no trace of limiting ignorance remains anywhere within it, at any level of the cosmic hierarchy. The source distinguishes this from what 'merely appears to be liberation' — the many partial, real-but-incomplete freedoms described in the preceding verses.\n\n**Beginner Note**\nThis is the finish line the last several verses were building toward: real liberation isn't just 'free from most of what was limiting me' — it's total, with absolutely nothing left over anywhere. Anything short of that, however genuine and valuable, is still only an approximation of the real thing.\n\n**Syncretic Application**\nIt's worth having some standard, even a demanding one, for what 'fully resolved' actually means for a given problem — otherwise it becomes too easy to mistake 'better than before' for 'actually done.' Naming the difference honestly, as this verse does, doesn't devalue partial progress; it just keeps you clear on which one you've actually reached."
